#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054355 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0e5efaa-e624-5045-abdb-cdf8d32ad6cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of a Given Set"}] :question/id #uuid "70291b7a-ae46-5e8a-b24b-3ea343eb84f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-13195" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subset of $S=\\{n \\in \\mathbb{Z}: {-3} \\leq 2n+1 \\leq 7 \\}?$\n\n- $\\{ -2, 0, 4 \\}$\n\n- $\\{ 0, 3 \\}$\n\n- $\\{ -3, 1, 3 \\}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the set $S.$ Solving the given inequality, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\le 2n+1 & \\le 7 \\\\ -4\\le 2n & \\le 6 \\\\ -2\\le n & \\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $S$ contains all integers between $-2$ and $3$ (including $-2$ and $3$):\n\n$$\nS = \\{ -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3 \\}\n$$\n\nA set is a subset of $S$ if all its elements are elements of $S.$ With that in mind, let's inspect each option.\n\n- $\\{ -2,0,4 \\}$ is *not* a subset of $S$ because it contains the element $4 \\notin S. \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\n- $\\{ 0,3 \\}$ is a subset of $S. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\n- $\\{ -3,1,3 \\}$ is *not* a subset of $S$ because it contains the element $-3 \\notin S. \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054356 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cbddaef-3828-5454-b892-26a3c6242ddb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding All Subsets of a Given Set"}] :question/id #uuid "0c50c147-4043-5aa0-b716-a9745206fdb4" :question/math-academy-id "e-13196" :question/problem "Find all of the subsets of $A = \\{ a, b, c \\}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find every subset of $A,$ we list out every combination of elements within $A.$ \n\nWe can use the following diagram to visualize the process:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038640/assets/8323a4292a4f06d66d456bfce1906cbc7e9933a3fac41dd5fb643e13d25f6a6e.png)\n\nNote that we must include the empty set since it is a subset of every set.\n\nBy doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\emptyset, \\{a\\}, \\{b\\}, \\{c\\}, \\{a, b\\}, \\{a, c\\}, \\{b, c\\}, \\{a, b, c\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054357 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b7f1d12-3335-5dd7-bf36-e07077d0c45d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of Special Sets"}] :question/id #uuid "f2840f6f-da8b-519d-a191-f19d37fa7957" :question/math-academy-id "e-13197" :question/problem "Which of the following are subsets of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+$ (the set of non-negative integers)?\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}$\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}^-$ (the set of negative integers)\n\n- $\\mathbb{N}$" :question/worked-solution "$A$ is a subset of $B$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect the given choices.\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}$ is *not* a subset of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+.$ For example, $-1 \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ but $-1 \\notin \\mathbb{Z}_0^+.$\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}^-$ is *not* a subset of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+.$ For example, $-1 \\in \\mathbb{Z}^-$ but $-1 \\notin \\mathbb{Z}_0^+.$\n\n- $\\mathbb{N}$ is a subset of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+.$ Every natural number is a positive integer.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054358 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "495f3da0-849a-589d-ac88-26d6825445e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Subsets"}] :question/id #uuid "e8db159d-7a1b-52ab-be96-6b1fd3eabb29" :question/math-academy-id "e-13198" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A =\\{ n \\in \\mathbb{N} \\mid 2n \\geq 10 \\}$ and $B= \\{ n\\in \\mathbb{Z} \\mid n+2 \\geq 5 \\}?$\n\n- $A\\subseteq B$\n\n- $B\\subseteq A$\n\n- $B\\nsubseteq A$\n\n- $B\\supseteq A$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the natural numbers that satisfy the inequality $2n\\geq 10,$ that is, $n\\geq 5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA= \\{5,6,7,8,9,\\ldots \\}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality $n+2\\geq 5,$ that is, $n\\geq 3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nB= \\{3,4,5,6,7,\\ldots\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A\\subseteq B$ is true. Every element of $A$ is an element of the set $B.$\n\n- $B\\subseteq A$ is false, while $B\\nsubseteq A$ is true. For example, $3 \\in B$ but $3 \\notin A.$\n\n- $B\\supseteq A$ is equivalent to $A \\subseteq B,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300887 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0e5efaa-e624-5045-abdb-cdf8d32ad6cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of a Given Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "604e7666-c088-5db4-a646-ca9915b74fb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300889} {:answer/id #uuid "d22977ce-d7b7-5ca0-a71e-194852923c26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300890} {:answer/id #uuid "30d85eb7-a8c4-58a7-87fc-94d97679f509" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300891} {:answer/id #uuid "c560448b-07eb-56d5-a262-a9439133be00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300892} {:answer/id #uuid "a0ee2eac-4135-5343-a2a6-6cb676dee9d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0ee2eac-4135-5343-a2a6-6cb676dee9d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300893} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8c7ba98-5908-5651-aef7-91409e5dbafc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4788deb7-7ef4-5a10-b588-c3a236a88d09" :question/math-academy-id "q-104916" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subset of $A={{12},{20,22},{6,9,15}}?$\n\n- ${-1,{12}}$\n- ${{20,22}}$\n- ${{0},{6,9,15}}$" :question/worked-solution "A set is a subset of $A$ if all its elements are elements of $A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each option.\n\n- ${-1,{12}}$ is *not* a subset of $A$ because it contains the element $-1\\notin A.\\,\\times $\n\n- ${{20,22}}$ is a subset of $A.\\,✓$\n\n- ${{0},{6,9,15}}$ is *not* a subset of $A$ because it contains the element ${0}\\notin A.\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300894 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cbddaef-3828-5454-b892-26a3c6242ddb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding All Subsets of a Given Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc2ff121-820b-52c2-b693-d6ed16de88ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{0},{1},{-1,0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300896} {:answer/id #uuid "24f5e149-b2c2-5e2d-96a1-353036ed393e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅,{0},{1},{0,1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300897} {:answer/id #uuid "f8fea61a-e1a7-5363-bed2-b0a8632c866b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{0},{-1},{1},{-1,1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300898} {:answer/id #uuid "0d2d551e-b3f4-522e-9001-14569ed6604a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{-1},{0,1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300899} {:answer/id #uuid "3bee4e3f-eba6-5c0e-8b3e-45c07d7bbf4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅,{1},{1,2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300900}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24f5e149-b2c2-5e2d-96a1-353036ed393e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅,{0},{1},{0,1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300897} :answer-field/id #uuid "9dff9a6a-b9cf-56f0-80c2-36ecdda2b744" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dfdf525d-068e-5e8a-a728-e4a1792f0ac6" :question/math-academy-id "q-104925" :question/problem "Find all of the subsets of $A={0,1}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find every subset of $A,$ we list out every combination of elements within $A.$ \n\nWe can use the following diagram to visualize the process:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038640/assets/bb31e2bd632f39706b2785ce6cab764673997fe77b8a9417f7dc213cdff4b7a7.png)\n\nNote that we must include the empty set, since it is a subset of every set.\n\nBy doing this, we get\n\n$$\n∅,{0},{1},{0,1}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300901 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b7f1d12-3335-5dd7-bf36-e07077d0c45d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of Special Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bc90cb7-4ec9-59f6-8401-434e866fc3c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300903} {:answer/id #uuid "cb5575f0-fea3-514f-ab3b-8c57659c577e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300904} {:answer/id #uuid "384948e2-1c4a-5bd4-a532-7818b6eaa82e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300905} {:answer/id #uuid "449ccb0a-e190-598c-86c9-5f68b2f0620a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300906} {:answer/id #uuid "98b55c16-84c3-522a-af9e-025a4a6a2af4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "449ccb0a-e190-598c-86c9-5f68b2f0620a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300906} :answer-field/id #uuid "03fb7170-22d7-5a2a-9194-b0dc2af3fcd2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300902}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7680f948-1688-5658-b595-3c37b74265b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-105034" :question/problem "Which of the following are subsets of $R?$\n\n- ${Q}^{-}$ (the set of negative rationals)\n- ${Z}^{+}$ (the set of positive integers)\n- $N$" :question/worked-solution "$A$ is a subset of $B$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect the given choices.\n\n- ${Q}^{-}$ is  a subset of $R.$ Every negative rational number is a real number.\n\n- ${Z}^{+}$ is a subset of $R.$ Every positive integer number is a real number.\n\n- $N$ a subset of $R.$ Every natural number is a real number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300908 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b7f1d12-3335-5dd7-bf36-e07077d0c45d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of Special Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e29efb3-1373-5e2e-8d00-795254c1eaa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q⊆Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300910} {:answer/id #uuid "c7982993-e896-5b93-9233-611859b5a7b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q⊆R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300911} {:answer/id #uuid "ea2f1c28-6275-5ddc-8751-ceafc1282548" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{Z}^{-}⊆N" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300912} {:answer/id #uuid "adef0a65-5cb8-52ba-8bf7-0637ee4acce7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N⊆C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300913} {:answer/id #uuid "881498c9-be6f-5efd-a005-ac512575f4a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Z⊆Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea2f1c28-6275-5ddc-8751-ceafc1282548" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{Z}^{-}⊆N" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300912} :answer-field/id #uuid "78636117-3097-5d38-a9ce-05be652b501a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "91d09261-286a-5e85-a61f-8483207f7bbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-105039" :question/problem "Which statement is **not** true?" :question/worked-solution "If $A⊆B,$ then $A$ is a subset of $B,$ meaning that every element of $A$ is an element of $B.$\n\nAmong the given choices, only ${Z}^{-}⊆N$ is not true. This statement is not true because not every negative integer is a natural number. For example, we have $-1\\in {Z}^{-}$ but $-1\\notin N.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300915 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "495f3da0-849a-589d-ac88-26d6825445e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Subsets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ddd387eb-ba17-5d21-b2de-737f5a252db5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300917} {:answer/id #uuid "2806b1b0-bb56-5070-b3ac-229501b50366" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300918} {:answer/id #uuid "2845a4bf-3b5e-5cf9-a985-fc686a662a91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300919} {:answer/id #uuid "02a66fd6-63a7-5f02-aac2-051a0cc4be24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300920} {:answer/id #uuid "52d1851e-405b-56ae-b75d-ff639bb4de5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2845a4bf-3b5e-5cf9-a985-fc686a662a91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300919} :answer-field/id #uuid "62b3f659-2e8b-5bf8-849c-7bf158e0d774" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "90026120-6d23-527b-8cd4-e6e4979a06e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-105064" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={n\\in N∣n-2\\ge 4}$ and $B={n\\in Z∣n+2\\ge 5}?$\n\n- $A⊆B$\n- $B⊆A$\n- $∅⊆A$\n- $B⊇A$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the natural numbers that satisfy the inequality $n-2\\ge 4,$ that is to say, $n\\ge 6.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA={6,7,8,9,10,11,…}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality $n+2\\ge 5,$ That is to say, $n\\ge 3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nB={3,4,5,6,7,8,…}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is true. Every element in the set $A$ is an element of the set $B.$\n\n- $B⊆A$ is false. For example, $3\\in B$ but $3\\notin A.$\n\n- $∅⊆A$ is true. The null set $∅={}$ is a subset of *every* set.\n\n- $B⊇A$ is equivalent to $A⊆B,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300922 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0e5efaa-e624-5045-abdb-cdf8d32ad6cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of a Given Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0e9f790-bfcc-533a-b74b-77581de111c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a,b,e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300924} {:answer/id #uuid "7ebe88fa-8dff-5ea9-9076-25a43b697150" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a,c,d}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300925} {:answer/id #uuid "748d67b5-9184-59a0-a3b3-02bffb7351a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300926} {:answer/id #uuid "de3baab5-2008-5199-9fff-be0bf937bb73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a,c,d,e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300927} {:answer/id #uuid "48c34e7d-d91b-5766-a153-ee547a1f1b9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a,b,c}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300928}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "748d67b5-9184-59a0-a3b3-02bffb7351a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300926} :answer-field/id #uuid "67ea5a84-a063-5f90-bc6c-ffaa2b20e4f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2e1f8f9b-8364-5550-a922-c34dacc07366" :question/math-academy-id "q-1187" :question/problem "Which set is a subset of $A={a,c,e}?$" :question/worked-solution "A set is a subset of $A$ if all its elements are elements of $A.$\n\nOf the given sets, only ${a}$ has all of its elements in $A.$\n\nTherefore, ${a}$ is a subset of $A.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300929 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0e5efaa-e624-5045-abdb-cdf8d32ad6cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of a Given Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d44517ee-3d6f-507f-8374-71773c43e79e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2,6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300931} {:answer/id #uuid "196cd7ad-5bf1-5c90-9fea-ae0a5e8795de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300932} {:answer/id #uuid "6805c159-44f8-5f1b-84c6-f9503dadd760" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300933} {:answer/id #uuid "59339c0a-97aa-5ed7-a9d7-2497c86de036" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300934} {:answer/id #uuid "0dabb692-b809-5f64-8d4b-add3d249ec5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{1,3,5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300935}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59339c0a-97aa-5ed7-a9d7-2497c86de036" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300934} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fe02cb1-6b45-55e8-9609-6c2856bed3a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300930}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4d9bbfa7-b5b1-52d4-bc25-0a500880f2e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-1189" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subset of $A={1,2,3,4}?$" :question/worked-solution "A set is a subset of $A$ if all its elements are elements of $A.$\n\nNotice that the empty set ${}$ is a subset of any set.\n\nTherefore, ${}$ is a subset of $A.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054889 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cbddaef-3828-5454-b892-26a3c6242ddb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding All Subsets of a Given Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e171efca-824b-5549-89b1-02ffcf234b4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅, {1}, {2}, {3}{1, 2, 3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051300} {:answer/id #uuid "0bac181b-09de-540e-8569-c6dccfea9c8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅, {1}, {2}, {3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051301} {:answer/id #uuid "19387d58-09aa-5c2d-b6af-70038f698a5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅, {1}, {2}, {3}, {1, 2}, {1, 3}, {2, 3}, {1, 2, 3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051302} {:answer/id #uuid "1a841690-0777-588c-abf9-2bd5037a07b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅, {1}, {2}, {3}, {1, 2}, {1, 3}, {2, 3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051303} {:answer/id #uuid "397e24bc-932d-53d0-a474-6dedf9690f6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{1}, {2}, {3}, {1, 2}, {1, 3}, {2, 3}, {1, 2, 3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051304}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19387d58-09aa-5c2d-b6af-70038f698a5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅, {1}, {2}, {3}, {1, 2}, {1, 3}, {2, 3}, {1, 2, 3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051302} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d0bd25e-209d-5419-930d-dfa7a7f63f40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051299}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5a3915aa-c1aa-5f3a-a352-0a780d0440c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-1192" :question/problem "Find all of the subsets of $T={1,2,3}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find every subset of $T,$ we list out every combination of elements within $T.$ \n\nWe can use the following diagram to visualize the process:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038640/assets/127ee1187ebf3a2cc498c88326285d5adf5d609cf3276885e8c9d525435133eb.png)\n\nNote that we must include the empty set since it is a subset of every set.\n\nBy doing this, we get\n\n$$\n∅,\\,{1},\\,{2},\\,{3},\\,{1,2},\\,{1,3},\\,{2,3},\\,{1,2,3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054900 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "495f3da0-849a-589d-ac88-26d6825445e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Subsets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cc24b2d-aefe-5550-8320-e73223adc725" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051366} {:answer/id #uuid "b21fb707-a876-58e3-8917-4477dbbc31bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051367} {:answer/id #uuid "ece9a89e-b697-565f-9db7-d7d4ea7f957d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051368} {:answer/id #uuid "3d705157-90e1-541c-bdbc-dbf6b491c161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051369} {:answer/id #uuid "429e9c42-0358-5a78-bf17-15026921143a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051370}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "429e9c42-0358-5a78-bf17-15026921143a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051370} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6d45657-6892-5416-b2b9-12620528d63a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051365}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0906e8f1-346b-507f-9289-d9fb6006311a" :question/math-academy-id "q-1210" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={x\\in Z:-2\\le x<6}$ and $B={x\\in N:x+1<6}?$\n\n- $∅⊆B$\n- $B⊆A$\n- $A⊈B$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all integer numbers greater than or equal to $-2$ but less than $6.$ So,\n\n$$\nA={-2,-1,0,1,2,3,4,5}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are natural numbers that satisfy the inequality $x+1<6.$ So,\n\n$$\nB={1,2,3,4}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $∅⊆B$ is true. The null set $∅={}$ is a subset of *every* set.\n\n- $B⊆A$ is true. Every element in the set $B$ is an element of the set $A.$\n\n- $A⊈B$ is true. For example, $-1\\in A$ but $-1\\notin B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055155 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0e5efaa-e624-5045-abdb-cdf8d32ad6cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of a Given Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "062bf92d-e0d5-557c-9fbf-d048fd89bb6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052603} {:answer/id #uuid "3e7dd4fb-8526-5447-a84c-195f64ad3ac3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052604} {:answer/id #uuid "597ca4b6-20ed-5203-8b1a-2530d60ecc0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052605} {:answer/id #uuid "6d106475-574b-5be5-aae1-dc8626822d02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052606} {:answer/id #uuid "7fa13c8f-18a0-584e-8bd1-3b2da42d2d9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "597ca4b6-20ed-5203-8b1a-2530d60ecc0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052605} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ea1f0ed-b812-544b-b263-f7588ed37784" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8e8eb5c8-f406-5cdf-99fb-6d6cbbcd6c06" :question/math-academy-id "q-37255" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subset of $A={{1},{2,3},{1,3,6}}?$\n\n- ${2,{1}}$\n- ${{2,3}}$\n- ${{-1},{1,3,6}}$" :question/worked-solution "A set is a subset of $A$ if all its elements are elements of $A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each option.\n\n- ${2,{1}}$ is *not* a subset of $A$ because it contains the element $2\\notin A.\\,\\times $\n\n- ${{2,3}}$ is a subset of $A.\\,✓$\n\n- ${{-1},{1,3,6}}$ is *not* a subset of $A$ because it contains the element ${-1}\\notin A.\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300936 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "495f3da0-849a-589d-ac88-26d6825445e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Subsets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50492bf0-6806-5ae6-a0bb-9740e4aa95dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300938} {:answer/id #uuid "eb2a9b2b-512b-50ac-911c-5baa1df70aab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300939} {:answer/id #uuid "a6051aed-845e-52ec-9b25-e9a8ee913aad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300940} {:answer/id #uuid "d350a45f-c83f-54ec-b6a5-57f2369bcbdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300941} {:answer/id #uuid "5080db1c-caf7-54c0-882d-6633c1dcbebb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300942}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6051aed-845e-52ec-9b25-e9a8ee913aad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300940} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d3be556-e704-5b99-909e-015533bd7c65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300937}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dccd9da3-50bd-5ed9-bb93-47775cde65de" :question/math-academy-id "q-37260" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={x\\in N:3\\le x<9}$ and $B={x\\in N:3x<10}?$\n\n- $A⊆B$\n- $B⊆A$\n- $∅⊆A$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all natural numbers greater than or equal to $3$ but less than $9.$ So,\n\n$$\nA={3,4,5,6,7,8}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are natural numbers which satisfy the inequality $3x<10.$ So,\n\n$$\nB={1,2,3}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is false. For example, $4\\in A$ but $4\\notin B.$\n\n- $B⊆A$ is false. For example, $1\\in B$ but $1\\notin A.$\n\n- $∅⊆A$ is true. The null set $∅={}$ is a subset of *every* set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300943 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0e5efaa-e624-5045-abdb-cdf8d32ad6cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of a Given Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5892d4db-ee17-5a9b-bb7d-4dabe3624e20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={0,1,2,4,6,8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300945} {:answer/id #uuid "87bb18dc-9899-5965-962b-0ce6916f989c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={-1,0,1,2,4,6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300946} {:answer/id #uuid "38af7345-898b-5fad-87ca-5b2b33366056" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={1,3,5,7,9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300947} {:answer/id #uuid "18a1bd9e-7f78-57c3-b75d-8ce5a365964f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={2,6,12,14,32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300948} {:answer/id #uuid "17e08c17-c0a4-5c01-92e5-c8cea1cf8431" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={2,4,6,7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300949}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18a1bd9e-7f78-57c3-b75d-8ce5a365964f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={2,6,12,14,32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300948} :answer-field/id #uuid "1864bcf5-a777-5135-8aef-787b82f38f91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300944}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2265169c-0d8b-562a-b72b-43f80dabce9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-37265" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subset of $A={2n∣n\\in N}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the set $A.$ The elements of the set \n$A$ are all the natural even numbers.\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nA={2,4,6,8,…}.\n$$\n\nNow, a subset of $A$ is a set whose elements are also elements of the set $A.$\n\nIn our case, $B={2,6,12,14,32}$ is a subset of $A.$\n\nIn the rest of the options, the set $B$ has elements that are not in $A,$ meaning that it is not a subset of $A.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300950 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "495f3da0-849a-589d-ac88-26d6825445e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Subsets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bd53d33-1c8e-534e-9ce6-4529042d038f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300952} {:answer/id #uuid "913cbce1-d5c3-528b-a007-ec79a69a2193" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300953} {:answer/id #uuid "63d1dcb1-9405-5992-ac49-781495952da9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300954} {:answer/id #uuid "28c111ac-4ff1-53f3-b75f-8dbc64b928af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300955} {:answer/id #uuid "cefdc396-cdb8-534a-94b9-3b014581773a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300956}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "913cbce1-d5c3-528b-a007-ec79a69a2193" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300953} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2cd607a-2d8a-59d9-925b-b70565bf262c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300951}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5f2ce395-36f1-5103-8a97-86f354e329cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-37267" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={5,10,15,20,25,30,35,…}$ and $B={5n∣n\\in N,3\\le n\\le 7}?$\n\n- $B⊆A$\n- $A⊆B$\n- $A⊈B$\n- $A⊇B$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the set $B.$ Substituting the values $n\\in {3,4,5,6,7}$ into the formula $5n,$ we get the following elements:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n=3 & \\,⇒\\,5(3)=15 \\\\ n=4 & \\,⇒\\,5(4)=20 \\\\ n=5 & \\,⇒\\,5(5)=25 \\\\ n=6 & \\,⇒\\,5(6)=30 \\\\ n=7 & \\,⇒\\,5(7)=35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nB={15,20,25,30,35}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $B⊆A$ is true. Every element in the set $B$ is an element of the set $A.$\n\n- $A⊆B$ is false, while $A⊈B$ is true. For example, $5\\in A$ but $5\\notin B.$\n\n- $A⊇B$ is equivalent to $B⊆A,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300957 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b7f1d12-3335-5dd7-bf36-e07077d0c45d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of Special Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d6c89f0-2a1a-5531-b5d8-831e882897bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300959} {:answer/id #uuid "e6865ba6-79cb-5696-ab09-d0be9337b7cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300960} {:answer/id #uuid "639e0609-5927-50e2-922f-0b5aa58cc005" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300961} {:answer/id #uuid "888a6d2c-da5e-5d94-b324-cd6d72385bc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300962} {:answer/id #uuid "a1d2d22b-0b13-5678-876c-b0cb42f1ec3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d6c89f0-2a1a-5531-b5d8-831e882897bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300959} :answer-field/id #uuid "2783eb9e-a3b8-546f-9d63-ec7ede85ec61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c9ea526-3175-579e-bce7-db2dce376c08" :question/math-academy-id "q-506" :question/problem "Which of the following are subsets of $R?$\n\n- ${Q}^{+}$ (the set of positive rationals)\n- ${Z}^{-}$ (the set of negative integers)\n- $N$" :question/worked-solution "$A$ is a subset of $B$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect the given choices.\n\n- ${Q}^{+}$ is  a subset of $R.$ Every positive rational number is a real number.\n\n- ${Z}^{-}$ is a subset of $R.$ Every negative integer number is a real number.\n\n- $N$ a subset of $R.$ Every natural number is a real number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300964 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cbddaef-3828-5454-b892-26a3c6242ddb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding All Subsets of a Given Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3627f33-89fc-5572-b724-f74af00e4126" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅,{-1,0,1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300966} {:answer/id #uuid "133f8274-a91d-5d42-a160-88045cc2ebc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅,{-1},{0},{1},{-1,0},{-1,1},{0,1},{-1,0,1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300967} {:answer/id #uuid "812be9c2-b120-5da3-9283-c8f04385e9be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅,{-1},{0},{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300968} {:answer/id #uuid "2c1271d8-14ed-5ace-9984-acdbce142baa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{-1},{0},{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300969} {:answer/id #uuid "455efb32-78af-5cc5-a696-29483f48a435" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{-1,0,1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300970}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "133f8274-a91d-5d42-a160-88045cc2ebc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅,{-1},{0},{1},{-1,0},{-1,1},{0,1},{-1,0,1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300967} :answer-field/id #uuid "c954d862-1a12-52e4-9075-f60366291614" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300965}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "17fc06b1-e354-5e82-b08b-7057469ae185" :question/math-academy-id "q-80806" :question/problem "Find all of the subsets of $T={-1,0,1}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find every subset of $T,$ we list out every combination of elements within $T.$ \n\nWe can use the following diagram to visualize the process:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038640/assets/03bdaa1b1033d662675e8b594fbc6ed2376b43dd71e28af7a8bf61d1ff17a012.png)\n\nNote that we must include the empty set since it is a subset of every set.\n\nBy doing this, we get\n\n$$\n∅,{-1},{0},{1},{-1,0},{-1,1},{0,1},{-1,0,1}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300971 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cbddaef-3828-5454-b892-26a3c6242ddb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding All Subsets of a Given Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #u