ddaef-3828-5454-b892-26a3c6242ddb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding All Subsets of a Given Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4dd47303-153d-53c9-8585-16b4eedb092f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{-\\pi },{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300980} {:answer/id #uuid "4ceeaee3-0de2-58b2-a573-99d13b8055a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅,{-\\pi ,\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300981} {:answer/id #uuid "15cf81d1-eb58-5d38-b739-c57b9a661a24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅,{-\\pi },{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300982} {:answer/id #uuid "9d3047d4-2fac-561d-98c5-8e63d74fe94f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅,{-\\pi },{\\pi },{-\\pi ,\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300983} {:answer/id #uuid "884a03de-7ffd-5a19-b4db-73c4a19e7aff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{-\\pi },{\\pi },{-\\pi ,\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300984}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d3047d4-2fac-561d-98c5-8e63d74fe94f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅,{-\\pi },{\\pi },{-\\pi ,\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300983} :answer-field/id #uuid "26ba15e2-1571-5139-a1fb-3c2b08335857" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "00bdd032-b9a1-5a22-aa27-2be14a896901" :question/math-academy-id "q-80809" :question/problem "Find all of the subsets of $U={-\\pi ,\\pi }.$" :question/worked-solution "To find every subset of $U,$ we list out every combination of elements within $U.$ \n\nWe can use the following diagram to visualize the process:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038640/assets/b0e483a2d10cba17fb2bd3bf0e78d7b4d2fc07b22dbb5c492564b998bbfce6fc.png)\n\nNote that we must include the empty set since it is a subset of every set.\n\nBy doing this, we get\n\n$$\n∅,{-\\pi },{\\pi },{-\\pi ,\\pi }.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300985 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b7f1d12-3335-5dd7-bf36-e07077d0c45d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of Special Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c5213fe-c035-5fec-a59a-ef4f9270f5e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q⊆R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300987} {:answer/id #uuid "85e6b4d8-a6ea-566f-9562-99e396f1006c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N⊆Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300988} {:answer/id #uuid "1e79add0-ea94-5476-a9be-ae0681788037" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Z⊆R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300989} {:answer/id #uuid "47cdfd95-3adc-55de-8dc8-6ffad711e645" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N⊆C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300990} {:answer/id #uuid "ac99c84f-92d3-5525-a082-91a3afffe877" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Z⊆N" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac99c84f-92d3-5525-a082-91a3afffe877" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Z⊆N" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300991} :answer-field/id #uuid "bda17121-15f8-5fcc-ae39-e0cf752e6d29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300986}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb01c25e-4ce1-591f-b1b8-8c295e973407" :question/math-academy-id "q-80810" :question/problem "Which statement is **not** true?" :question/worked-solution "If $A⊆B,$ then $A$ is a subset of $B,$ meaning that every element of $A$ is an element of $B.$\n\nAmong the given choices, only $Z⊆N$ is not true. This statement is not true because not every integer is a natural number.  For example, we have $-1\\in Z$ but $-1\\notin N.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300992 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b7f1d12-3335-5dd7-bf36-e07077d0c45d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of Special Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5204b6b-e656-508f-b2f4-53c124882bb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300994} {:answer/id #uuid "97ee8bc7-80dc-5894-837c-6111723e5693" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300995} {:answer/id #uuid "09668d25-9b8f-5542-abcf-265eeba168aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300996} {:answer/id #uuid "051356c2-a92a-55b2-aef2-e1792c93ce19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300997} {:answer/id #uuid "e939d068-7d4e-56c3-837c-777d90860410" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300998}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e939d068-7d4e-56c3-837c-777d90860410" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300998} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d28e25c-b5bf-5439-a808-108dc7be28d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300993}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bc2578ed-7aae-5f12-a163-5aa37a8919bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-80812" :question/problem "Which of the following are subsets of ${Q}^{+}$  (the set of positive rational numbers)?\n\n- ${Q}_{0}^{+}$ (the set of non-negative rational numbers)\n- $N$\n- $Z$" :question/worked-solution "$A$ is a subset of $B$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect the given choices.\n\n- ${Q}_{0}^{+}$ is not a subset of ${Q}^{+}.$ For example, $0\\in {Q}_{0}^{+}$ but $0\\notin {Q}^{+}.$\n\n- $N$ is a subset of ${Q}^{+}.$ Indeed, every natural number is also a positive rational number.\n\n- $Z$ is not a subset of ${Q}^{+}.$ For example, $-1\\in Z$ but $-1\\notin {Q}^{+}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300999 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "495f3da0-849a-589d-ac88-26d6825445e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Subsets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07cefd1a-a90f-5344-b0af-d3883e1ae213" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301001} {:answer/id #uuid "f1fd0f94-107a-5513-91c7-7553805f2142" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301002} {:answer/id #uuid "5d408e83-9890-59fd-937d-71c5c9bf1370" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301003} {:answer/id #uuid "ece62413-ee86-5be4-bebf-0e7588e28923" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301004} {:answer/id #uuid "8284ced4-6a6f-5a6f-8ede-4dff48224ea7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301005}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8284ced4-6a6f-5a6f-8ede-4dff48224ea7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301005} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa18f640-0d06-58ad-a0c0-856ff8c6e42a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a90c52c-818b-5155-a8eb-42e1d51e8eb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-80845" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={n\\in Z∣n+1\\ge -1}$ and $B={n\\in Z∣n-2\\ge -4}?$\n\n- $A⊆B$\n- $B⊆A$\n- $B⊈A$\n- $B⊇A$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the integer numbers that satisfy the inequality $n+1\\ge -1,$ that is to say, $n\\ge -2.$ So, we have \n\n$$\nA={-2,-1,0,1,2,3,4,5,6,…}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality $n-2\\ge -4,$ That is to say, $n\\ge -2.$ So, we have \n\n$$\nB={-2,-1,0,1,2,3,4,5,6,…}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is true. Every element in the set $A$ is an element of the set $B.$\n\n- $B⊆A$ is true, while $B⊈A$ is false. Every element in the set $B$ is an element of the set $A.$\n\n- $B⊇A$ is equivalent to $A⊆B,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and IV only\"."}]}
